<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Daigo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/884">884</a>–930</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Daigo-tennō, Minamoto no Korezane, Atsuhito</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Daigo was the 60th emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> according to the traditional order of succession, reigning from 897 to 930. The eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-uda">Emperor Uda</a>, he was born a subject named Minamoto no Korezane, because his grandfather Emperor Kōkō had demoted his sons from the imperial ranks, and became an imperial prince only in 887. The highlight of his 34-year reign was that he ruled by himself without a Fujiwara regent, though he was part Fujiwara himself; in his reign Ki no Tsurayuki presented the Kokin Wakashū. Falling ill in 930, he abdicated, became a monk and died, and was buried at Daigo-ji, which gave him his posthumous name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-daigo.jpg" alt="Emperor Daigo" width="330" height="660" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_Daigo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kanmu and his successors down to Emperor Saga governed in person, and <b>Emperor Daigo</b> ruled directly from 897 to 930.</blockquote><a href="/societies/heian-japan">Social Hierarchy of Heian Japan</a></li></ul></section>
